## Architecture and flow diagrams (required for solution analysis)

When a page compares **two or more candidate schemes** (e.g. Scheme A vs B), **each scheme must have its own architecture section** with diagrams — not prose-only summaries.

### Required per scheme

1. **System / architecture diagram** — ASCII or structured `<pre>` block showing components, queues, threads/modules, and data flow (entry → queue → handler → downstream).
2. **Indication or process flow diagram** — step-by-step delivery path (e.g. BAL RX → transport → module queue → callback → side effects).
3. **Symmetric layout** — use the same section template for every candidate so readers can compare side-by-side (see §4.1 / §4.2 on the reference page).

### Section template (design / feasibility reports)

```
<h2>N. Target Architectures</h2>
<h3>N.1 Scheme A — … Detailed</h3>
  [system diagram]
  [delivery flow diagram]
  [subscription / routing rules]
  [warnings: queue-full, pool, etc.]
<h3>N.2 Scheme B — … Detailed</h3>
  [same structure as N.1]
```

- Mark the **recommended** scheme with a status label (e.g. `<span data-type="status" data-color="green">Recommended</span>`).
- A comparison **table alone is not sufficient** — diagrams are mandatory for each scheme being evaluated.
- When presenting solution analysis in chat before publishing, include equivalent ASCII diagrams or Mermaid flowcharts so the user can review structure before Confluence upload.

---

## Content patterns by document type

### Design / feasibility reports

Include, when applicable:

- Problem statement table (dimension vs current state)
- Ordering or causal-chain requirements (diagram or preformatted block)
- **Target architectures** — per-scheme system diagram + delivery/process flow (see **Architecture and flow diagrams** above); symmetric §N.1 / §N.2 layout
- **Candidate comparison** — side-by-side table for alternatives (e.g. Scheme A / B), not prose-only
- Shared-state / constraint table
- Phased roadmap (Phase 0, 1, 2…)
- Feasibility verdict table with status labels (Feasible / Partial / Not viable)
- Design checklist before implementation
- Key code locations table (file, line range, relevance)

### Flow / runbook pages (e.g. RSSI paths)

- Dual-path or multi-path comparison table
- Sequence from trigger → handler → side effect
- Code references with `file` and approximate line numbers

---

## Formatting (MCP `updateConfluencePage`)

- Use **`contentFormat: "html"`** for structured content (headings, tables, panels, code blocks, TOC macro).
- **Panels:** `<div data-type="panel-info|note|warning|success|error"><p>…</p></div>`
- **TOC macro:** `<div data-type="extension" data-extension-key="toc" …>` (see above)
- **Status:** `<span data-type="status" data-color="green|yellow|red|blue|neutral|purple">Label</span>`
- **Code:** `<pre><code class="language-c">…</code></pre>` (set language: `c`, `plaintext`, etc.)
- **Tables:** standard `<table><thead>…</thead><tbody>…</tbody></table>` — for **content**, not for TOC
- Do not wrap body in `<html>`, `<head>`, or `<body>` tags.

### Version message

Always set a short **`versionMessage`** on update (e.g. `Add TOC macro; expand Scheme A/B/C comparison`).

---

## Update workflow

1. **Read** current page: `getConfluencePage(cloudId, pageId, contentFormat: "html")`.
2. **Preserve** existing content when extending; keep the TOC macro after the scope panel.
3. **Update:** `updateConfluencePage` with full body, title, `versionMessage`.
4. Confirm version number incremented in the response.

**cloudId:** `vecima.atlassian.net`

---

## What to omit or fold in

- Standalone “FAQ / misconception” sections that only clarify one API detail — fold into the section where the reader needs it (e.g. `pon_ni_mask` note inside Candidate Architectures, not a separate §2).
- Duplicate Scheme B overview if a detailed deep-dive section exists — cross-reference by section number instead.
- **Manual TOC tables** — always use the TOC macro instead.
